PROBLEM:
- When an object can have many optional features / add-ons (pizza toppings, coffee extras, ice-cream toppings),
  using inheritance creates a large number of subclasses for every combination.
- This is called class explosion.
- We need a way to add features at runtime (dynamically) without modifying the existing classes.

SOLUTION:
1) Uses composition + inheritance/interfaces
   Decorator DP primarily uses composition (wrapping one object inside another).
   It also uses inheritance or interface implementation so that all objects share the same type.-First differentiate between Base and extra toppings/add-ons of the product.
2) The main-object/Base or the actual item that is being sold, that is our interface.
   Example: Pizza would be the end product, no matter what kind of it is. And then it is an interface, while the types of
   pizza are the implementation of Pizza interface.
   Because if we add anything on pizza/iceCream at the end final object remains pizza/iceCream itself.
   So, Base product is interface.
3) Else the remaining things like toppings/add-ons to that will be the classes which implements the interface.
   Because we wrap the toppings over Base product. Wrapping logic is shared.
4) Create an abstract class of decorator/add-ons, abstract because every decorator has its own implementation, its own features.
   Decorator will also wrap Base object, so decorator will implement Base interface.
5) We create different classes for different add-ons and they will be the child classes of Decorator-class.
6) Now in future if we any new add-on/feature will be there that will be introduced in a new class, which is a child
   class of decorator class.


For every new feature create a decorator class of it and than implement the variations of same feature as a child class of it.